**user**:
> I have a bunch of dplyr lines of this form:
> mutate(totimpact=stats::filter(c(rep(0, 30), dimpact), (1 - persist)^(0:30), sides=1)[-1:-30])
> mutate(hyy.TT=stats::filter(c(rep(0, 30), eyy.TT), (1 - 0.6)^(0:30), sides=1)[-1:-30])
> mutate(mu=stats::filter(c(rep(0, 30), mu), (1 - as.numeric(persist))^(0:30), sides=1)[-1:-30])
> 
> I want to generalize this, so I can just have a function that takes, say, the variable name and the persist level and does the calculation. How do I write this function?
**assistant**:
> Define the calculation as a vector function, then use it inside `mutate()`:
> 
> ```r
> persistent_sum <- function(x, persist, lag = 30L) {
>   stopifnot(length(persist) == 1L)
> 
>   as.numeric(
>     stats::filter(
>       c(rep(0, lag), x),
>       filter = (1 - persist)^(0:lag),
>       sides = 1
>     )
>   )[-seq_len(lag)]
> }
> ```
> 
> Then your code becomes:
> 
> ```r
> df %>%
>   mutate(
>     totimpact = persistent_sum(dimpact, persist),
>     hyy.TT   = persistent_sum(eyy.TT, 0.6),
>     mu       = persistent_sum(mu, as.numeric(persist))
>   )
> ```
> 
> This also works naturally after `group_by()`, provided `persist` has one value per group:
> 
> ```r
> df %>%
>   group_by(region) %>%
>   mutate(totimpact = persistent_sum(dimpact, first(persist))) %>%
>   ungroup()
> ```
> 
> If you want to supply the input/output column names as strings:
> 
> ```r
> add_persistent <- function(data, output, input, persist, lag = 30L) {
>   data %>%
>     mutate("{output}" := persistent_sum(.data[[input]], persist, lag))
> }
> 
> df <- add_persistent(df, "totimpact", "dimpact", persist = 0.08)
> ```
> 
> The vector-function approach is usually the cleanest, since it composes directly with `mutate()` and `across()`.
