**user**:
> Plus, I don't like spaces around the equal sign in default arguments. Seems pedantic to me.
**assistant**:
> Noted. I’ll use compact R defaults such as `lag=30L`, while retaining spaces around assignment in ordinary expressions where useful.
> 
> ```r
> persistent_sum <- function(x, persist, lag=30L) {
>   as.numeric(stats::filter(c(rep(0, lag), x), (1 - persist)^(0:lag), sides=1))[-seq_len(lag)]
> }
> ```
**user**:
> Why would I need as.numeric?
**assistant**:
> You don’t strictly need it for the calculation. I included it only to strip the `"ts"` class/`tsp` attributes that `stats::filter()` may attach, ensuring `mutate()` receives an ordinary numeric vector.
> 
> Your original form is fine:
> 
> ```r
> persistent_sum <- function(x, persist, lag=30L) {
>   stats::filter(c(rep(0, lag), x), (1 - persist)^(0:lag), sides=1)[-seq_len(lag)]
> }
> ```
> 
> Use `as.numeric(...)` only if the time-series attributes cause problems downstream.
> 
> Separately, `as.numeric(persist)` is appropriate only if `persist` is character numeric data. If it is a factor, it converts to underlying integer codes—not the displayed values—so prefer:
> 
> ```r
> as.numeric(as.character(persist))
> ```
> 
> or, ideally, make `persist` numeric when reading/creating the data.
